00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:05.520 align:start position:0%
anger outside police headquarters in  london  a day after a vigil ended in scenes that  shocked many people here in the uk  demonstrators gathered mixing demands  for an end to male violence against  women  with claims that the police are part of  the problem at saturday's vigil the  focus was clearly  on women victims of male violence  here the focus has shifted with  denunciation of police brutality  hundreds had come to this south london  vigil near where sarah everard went  missing  initially police kept their distance but  their decision to move in on the crowds  and their use of force to arrest women  created outrage  home secretary pretty patel asked the  metropolitan police force for a full  report  the department said there were  unanswered questions after she met the  met chief cressida dick  and london's mayor sadiq khan from the  opposition labour party  says he's unsatisfied with her account  insisting the met  didn't honor its assurances the vigil  would be policed sensitively  but despite calls from some for cressida  dick to resign she's standing firm and  strongly defending what happened  we had a really big crowd that gathered  lots of speeches and  quite rightly as far as i can see my  team  felt this is now an unlawful gathering  which poses a considerable risk to  people's health  according to the regulations a really  invidious position for my officers to  find themselves in  labor is now set to vote against a new  policing bill in the coming days  this week in parliament we start a  policing bill  300 or so pages 170 something clauses  and almost nothing  almost nothing if you can believe it on  violence against women and girls  the gaping hole in this bill is why  we'll be voting against it  and a complete missed opportunity by the  government civil rights groups have also  criticized the legislation saying it  gives police too many powers to ban  demonstrations if they'll cause  disruption  more protests are planned near  parliament on monday as the pressure on  the capitals police force  and the politicians it answers to  increases  nadine barbara al jazeera london
 

